course: dictionary
lesson: 7646
title: Progressive: step by step
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Progressive comes from progress, which means moving forward. Something progressive happens step by step over time, not all at once. «A progressive tax takes a bigger share from people who earn more.» The word shows a change that builds up gradually.
explain: Context helps again. «The simple present ("I run") is used for habits and things that are generally true; the present progressive ("I am running") is for something happening right now.» The words around progressive, such as present and "am running", tell you it is a grammar name for an action that is going on at this moment.
explain: Progressive can also describe a change that gets worse bit by bit, as in a progressive illness. «The progressive forms use a form of "be" plus a verb ending in -ing: "I am playing" (present), "I was playing" (past).» The clues are "be" and "-ing", so you can spot the pattern. Ask yourself: is the action still going on?
explain: Words can also describe ideas. Progressive ideas support new ideas and changes in society, especially to make things fairer. In history, progressive reformers fought for votes for women and an end to child labour. The meaning is still about moving forward, just in a fairness sense.
fact: Progressive means happening or developing step by step over time.
fact: The progressive form of a verb shows an action going on, using «be» plus a verb ending in -ing, like «I am running» or «she was singing».
fact: The present progressive, also called the present continuous, is am/is/are + -ing, for something happening right now.
fact: A progressive illness gets gradually worse over time.
fact: A progressive tax takes a bigger share from people who earn more.
exercise: «I am playing in the park.» Which tense is shown by «am playing»? | Future simple | Simple present | Simple past | *Present progressive
exercise: A progressive illness does what? | *Gets gradually worse | Disappears at once | Stays exactly the same | Gets better in one day
exercise: Mia says progressive means sudden and quick. Is she right? | No, it means not moving at all | Yes, it means happening all at once | Yes, it means happening only at night | *No, it means happening step by step over time
test: «She was reading when the phone rang.» Which form shows the action was going on? | reads | *was reading | read | will read
test: A progressive tax takes what from people who earn more? | A smaller share | *A bigger share | Nothing at all | A share only once a year
exam: Which sentence uses the present progressive for something happening right now? | I walk to school every day | *I am walking to school now | I walked to school yesterday | I will walk to school tomorrow
